# HTTP & HTTPS Fundamentals

## HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol)
- Used when you visit websites
- Developed by Tim Berners-Lee (1989–1991)
- Set of rules for communication between client (browser) and servers
- Transfers data like:
  - HTML
  - Images
  - Videos
- Data is not encrypted (insecure)
 
## HTTPS (HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure)
- Secure version of HTTP   
- Data is encrypted during transmission
- Prevents attacks like:
  - Reading data (confidentiality)
  - Modifying data (integrity)
- Ensures authentication (correct server)

# URL (Uniform Resource Locator)

## Definition
- Instruction to access a resource on the internet
- Tells browser how and where to get data

## Components of URL

### Scheme (Protocol)
- Defines protocol (HTTP, HTTPS, FTP)

### User Info
- Username and password (rarely used)

### Host (Domain/IP)
- Server address (e.g., example.com)

### Port
- Connection port
- Default:
  - 80 → HTTP
  - 443 → HTTPS

### Path
- Location of resource on server

### Query String
- Extra data sent (e.g., ?id=1)

### Fragment
- Specific section of a page (#section)

# Making an HTTP Request

## Request Structure
- Example:
  - GET / HTTP/1.1

## Request Parts

### Request Method
- GET → Fetch data

### Path
- / → Homepage

### HTTP Version
- HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2

### Headers (Extra Info)
- Host → Target website
- User-Agent → Browser info
- Referer → Previous page URL

### Blank Line
- Indicates end of request

# Example Request Breakdown

- Line 1 → GET request using HTTP/1.1
- Line 2 → Host (domain)
- Line 3 → User-Agent (browser info)
- Line 4 → Referer (previous page)
- Line 5 → Blank line (end of request)

# HTTP Response

## Definition
- Server replies with data + status

## Status Line
- Example:
  - HTTP/1.1 200 OK
- 200 → Success

## Response Headers
- Server → Server software
- Date → Response time
- Content-Type → Type of data (HTML, JSON)
- Content-Length → Size of data

## Body
- Actual content (HTML page)

## Blank Line
- Marks end of headers

# HTTP Methods

- GET → Read
- POST → Create
- PUT → Update
- DELETE → Delete

# HTTP Status Codes

## Categories

- 100–199 → Informational
- 200–299 → Success
- 300–399 → Redirection
- 400–499 → Client Errors
- 500–599 → Server Errors

## Common Status Codes

### Success
- 200 OK → Request successful
- 201 Created → New resource created

### Redirection
- 301 Moved Permanently → Resource moved permanently
- 302 Found → Temporary redirect

### Client Errors
- 400 Bad Request → Invalid request
- 401 Unauthorized → Authentication required
- 403 Forbidden → Access denied
- 404 Not Found → Resource not found
- 405 Method Not Allowed → Wrong HTTP method

### Server Errors
- 500 Internal Server Error → Server failed
- 503 Service Unavailable → Server overloaded/maintenance

# HTTP Headers

## Definition
- Extra data sent with requests and responses
- Helps client and server communicate properly

## Common Request Headers

- Host → Website name
- User-Agent → Browser name + version
- Content-Length → Size of data sent
- Accept-Encoding → Compression supported (gzip, etc.)
- Cookie → Stores user data

## Common Response Headers

- Set-Cookie → Sends cookies to browser
- Cache-Control → Defines caching rules
- Content-Type → Type of response data
- Content-Encoding → Compression used

# Cookies

## Definition
- Small pieces of data stored in browser

## Working

- Client sends request
- Server responds with Set-Cookie
- Browser stores cookie
- Future requests send cookie back
- Server पहचान… oops sorry… recognizes user 😏

## Key Points

- Helps:
  - Identify user
  - Store preferences
  - Maintain login sessions
- Data is:
  - Not stored as plain passwords
  - Stored as tokens (random strings)
- Used mainly for:
  - Authentication
  - Session management
